    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_4d637c47f4363c925e85aeac79843b9ab153fe26108e5fc42d2bdc963ee60b37",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "Repeated local averaging constructs a cutoff that is flat on most of the target interval yet has controlled derivatives and a product-sinc Fourier transform with tunable tail decay. That cutoff can be inserted to replace the finite t-range by whole-line Fourier mean squares and separate the complementary prime sums; only after this separation do comparison lemmas replace the cutoff transform by simpler sine kernels with explicit boundary errors.",
