    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_9cb0712ea3d4ebd1ee93cde7fa86e881d1f74458180a1d20ea9519d85034b14a",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "The detailed formula near x=T acts as a bridge because its oscillatory and cumulative-error terms simplify differently according to the ratio T/x. The below-T treatment bounds those terms through f, while the above-T treatment replaces small-argument sine factors, evaluates full integrals, and cancels constants; these two reductions recover the respective endpoint forms that are then combined with the far-range strong-pair-correlation proposal in a three-branch conjecture.",
